<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel Ángel Asturias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel Ángel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> (1899–1974) was a Guatemalan novelist, poet and diplomat who received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1967. His novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-senor-presidente">El Señor Presidente</a>, published in 1946, portrayed life under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Manuel Estrada Cabrera</a>, while Men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a> and his banana trilogy drew on Maya myth and attacked the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>. He spent long periods in exile and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miguel-angel-asturias.jpg" alt="Miguel Ángel Asturias" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dominique Roger · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miguel_Angel_Asturias.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miguel Ángel Asturias was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> on 19 October 1899, the first child of Ernesto Asturias Girón, a lawyer and judge, and María Rosales de Asturias, a schoolteacher. In 1905 the family moved to the house of his grandparents. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in the 1920s, where he studied ethnology at the Sorbonne with Georges Raynaud, an expert on the Quiché Maya, and finished a translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a> in 1926. He also associated with the Surrealists, and some scholars credit him with bringing features of modernist style, such as magical realism, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> writing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> was involved in politics throughout his career, opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Manuel Estrada Cabrera</a> and served as ambassador in several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries. He supported the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a>, whose policies ran against the interests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>. When Árbenz fell in 1954, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-castillo-armas">Carlos Castillo Armas</a> expelled Asturias and stripped him of his citizenship. He spent eight years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, where he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. In 1966 he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s Lenin Peace Prize, and in 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature, which some critics thought was awarded to a writer little known outside Latin America. He spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and died there on 9 June 1974.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first book, Leyendas de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> (1930), retells Maya myths. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-senor-presidente">El Señor Presidente</a>, completed in 1933 and privately published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1946, denounces the dictator Estrada Cabrera, and has been called a study of fear. Hombres de maíz (Men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a>, 1949) tells of an indigenous community whose land is threatened by planters and sets the Maya belief that the first humans were made of maize against the modernising society around them. The banana trilogy, Viento fuerte (1950), El Papa Verde (1954) and Los ojos de los enterrados (1960), is a fictional account of foreign control of the banana industry and the exploitation of native workers, and it earned him the Lenin Peace Prize. Mulata de tal followed in 1963.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, himself a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a>, proposed a hybrid national identity for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, ladino in language and Maya in mythology. His prose, rhythmic and rich in repetition and onomatopoeia, has been called tropical baroque. The Miguel Ángel Asturias National Prize in Literature, the country's most distinguished literary prize, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel Ángel Asturias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The K’iche’ activist Rigoberta Menchú received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1992, and the novelist <b>Miguel Ángel Asturias</b>, whose El Señor Presidente portrays a dictatorship modelled on that of Estrada Cabrera, the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1967.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Guatemala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Miguel Ángel Asturias"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4958 3761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4958 3761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
